  url: /genova/santo-stefano-terrace-monumental-bridge
  title: Santo Stefano Terrace & Monumental Bridge
  excerpt: >
    A hidden terrace above Via XX Settembre where Romanesque elegance meets Liberty-era grandeur:
    admire the striped marble façade of Santo Stefano, the dramatic Palace of Giants, and the busy arches
    of the Mercato Orientale. Don't miss the secret stairway or the cyclist’s shortcut.
  author: Luca / Bike Fever
  datetime: auto
  coords:
    lat: 44.40634
    lon: 8.93894
  details:
    history:
      - "Santo Stefano Church (c.972), Romanesque icon with marble and slate stripes, linked to baptisms of Columbus and Balilla"
      - "Ponte Monumentale (1890–95) by Cesare Gamba, replacing Porta degli Archi; monumental archway 21 m high"
      - "Palace of Giants (1896) by Carbone & Fuselli, with 8 reinforced concrete Atlantes, first of its kind in Genoa"
    architecture:
      - "Romanesque façade with striped marble, gothic‑Roman dome (1306)"
      - "Liberty‑style palazzi nearby: Cupole, Hotel Bristol, Orzali, Pastorino, showcasing early 20th‑century urban renewal"
    market:
      - "Mercato Orientale (1899): first reinforced concrete building in Genoa (Hennebique); 5 500 m², 42 storage cellars, 360 m colonnades, marble décor"
    experiences:
      - "Hidden staircase outside the right side of the nave (facing altar) leads up to Ponte Monumentale for a stunning view"
      - "Cyclist’s tip: to access the terrace by bike, ride through Via delle Casacce, then turn into Via Lorenzo Garaventa leading directly to the piazzale"
      - "On foot: under the church's portico you'll find a steep staircase heading up to the terrace"
      - "The terrace offers layered perspectives: medieval church, Liberty palaces, Castelluccio vista toward Levante"
